  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.12760v1 [cs.LG] 16 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Allen Schmaltz  </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">allen@re.express</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Reexpress AI</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a more robust and interpretable formulation of the standard softmax activation function commonly used with neural networks by adding <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</span> (i.e., correctly predicted depth-matches into training) awareness and <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</span>-to-training-distribution awareness to the existing output <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</span> (i.e., decision-boundary) awareness. When used as the final-layer activation with language models, the resulting <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</span>-<span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</span>-<span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span>) activation function is more robust than the softmax function to co-variate shifts and out-of-distribution inputs in high-probability regions, and provides interpretability-by-exemplar via dense matching. Complementing the prediction-conditional estimates, the <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span> activation enables a partitioning of the class-wise empirical CDFs to guard against low class-wise recall among selective classifications. These properties make it preferable for selective classification, even when considering post-hoc calibration methods over the softmax.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large neural-network-based language models (LLMs) pose a challenge for interpretable and reliable deployment given the non-identifiability of their parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang &amp; Ding, 1997</a>, inter alia)</cite><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Informally, this means that two or more distinct sets of values for the parameters can result in identical output distributions. As a consequence, interpreting the parameters of such models is typically much more complicated than with a simple linear regression model, for example.</span></span></span>, which can number in the billions or more. Instead of directly interpreting parameters, we might instead consider moving the focus of interpretation to auditing predictions as a form of interpretability by example, or <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exemplar</span>, over the representation space of such models via dense matching. However, for real-world deployments, robust approaches for predictive uncertainty are also needed, both for human decision-making and for constructing sequentially dependent LLM pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Known theoretical results limit the statistical quantities that can be derived over LLMs. Statistical assurances in the distribution-free setting are limited to approximately conditional quantities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Valiant, 1984</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lei &amp; Wasserman, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foygel Barber et al., 2020</a>, inter alia)</cite>. Further, even typical approximately conditional quantities can be difficult to obtain in practice, since the minimal assumption of exchangeability with a known held-out data set is itself often violated with co-variate and label shifts, which can be difficult to foresee with existing methods. Epistemologically, the prevalence of hallucinations and highly-confident wrong answers with widely deployed LLMs suggests a technical impasse in effectively modeling the predictive uncertainty, despite significant work from Bayesian, Frequentist, and empirically motivated perspectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gal &amp; Ghahramani, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakshminarayanan et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ovadia et al., 2019</a>, inter alia)</cite>. A foundational piece is evidently missing from the picture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given these intrinsic challenges, we approach the problem of uncertainty quantification over LLMs from a new angle and ask: <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we leverage the metric learning and dense matching capabilities of neural networks over high-dimensional inputs to at least aim to maximize, with minimal distributional assumptions, the separation of aleatoric (irreducible) uncertainty and epistemic (reducible) uncertainty, decomposing the sources of the latter in a manner that is interpretable and actionable?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We answer this question in the affirmative with a conceptually parsimonious, LLM-driven partitioning of the data to decompose sources of epistemic uncertainty: Correctly predicted depth-matches into the training set (<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>), the <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math> to the training set, and the distance to the decision-boundary (<math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>). We use these signals to construct a new activation function, the <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation, which can be used as a replacement for the standard <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation, as, for example, the final-layer activation. The <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation enables more reliable estimates of the predictive uncertainty for selective classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow, 1957</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geifman &amp; El-Yaniv, 2017</a>, inter alia)</cite>, which addresses the need for uncertainty quantification with LLMs as part of multi-stage decision pipelines, in settings subject to co-variate shifts and out-of-distribution inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, in this work:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math>) activation function, which encodes strong signals of epistemic uncertainty, to replace the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We specifically examine the behavior of the <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation as a final-layer activation over language models for selective classification. Even when considering typical post-hoc calibration methods, the <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation is more robust than the <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> activation to co-variate-shifted and out-of-distribution inputs, ceteris paribus, while remaining informative over in-distribution data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce an approach for controlling the recall of selective classifications, based on a data-driven partitioning of the class-wise CDFs via the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation. Combined with the empirically-robust prediction-conditional estimates from the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation, this results in a straightforward, and empirically achievable, quantity of interest for selective classification.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Setting</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the standard multi-class classification setting (e.g., predicting the binary sentiment of a movie review). We are given a training dataset, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit=\{({\bm{x}}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit=\{({\bm{x}}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of inputs, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, paired with their corresponding ground-truth discrete labels, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a labeled calibration dataset, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, drawn from the same distribution as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We are then given a new test instance, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, from an unlabeled test set, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, and seek to estimate the label with a prediction, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, via the un-normalized log probabilities (“logits”, informally) of a final linear layer: <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}={\bm{W}}^{T}{\bm{h}}+{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}={\bm{W}}^{T}{\bm{h}}+{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}=\underlyingNetwork({\bm{x}};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>network</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}=\underlyingNetwork({\bm{x}};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the final hidden state of a network parameterized by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. The discrete prediction is taken as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; however, for learning <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for human decision-making, we also seek an estimate of the predictive uncertainty, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\,|\,{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\,|\,{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is typically obtained by normalizing <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> via the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation described next. We will make a distinction between models, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> (defined by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and when applicable, the exemplar adaptor, described below), which produce the prediction, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and estimators, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which provide an estimate of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\,|\,{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\,|\,{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, because different estimators can be used over the same model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> activation is commonly used in neural network architectures, including, for example, as a router in self-attention mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and mixture-of-experts models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, and forming the basis of the cross-entropy loss used for next-token training of LLMs. It is the typical final output layer of LLMs, converting the un-normalized model logits to a normalized probability distribution:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\softmax({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C,\tau\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\softmax({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C,\tau\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The inverse-temperature parameter, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, controls the sharpness of the distribution. As <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> converges to a uniform distribution where each class has probability <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the output converges to a distribution in which all of the mass is assigned to a single class. In deep learning, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a learnable, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">global</span> hyper-parameter; <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance-wise</span> variation in the distance to the decision-boundary is thus determined by the relative <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This model is learned by minimizing the cross-entropy loss between <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the index of the true labels over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural</span> logarithm of the loss is the counterpart to the base <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta,{\bm{W}},{\bm{b}};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{e}\left(\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta,{\bm{W}},{\bm{b}};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{e}\left(\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methods</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we revisit Eq. <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and  <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We seek to decouple the sources of epistemic uncertainty via a new activation function that is conceptually:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{c}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{c}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with a corresponding negative log likelihood loss that takes into account the change of base (§ <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activation Functions ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). Unique to this setting, a modification to label-conditional conformal prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vovk et al., 2005</a>)</cite> then follows via a parsimonious partitioning of the class-wise empirical CDFs, providing a principled basis for controlling the recall among selective classifications, combined with empirically-robust prediction-conditional estimates.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activation Functions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calculating the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation involves training an exemplar adaptor, a 1-D CNN adaptor (with a final linear layer) over the frozen hidden states of a network, to induce distilled, compressed representations of the underlying network’s representation space conditional on its predictions. The resulting representations provide a probabilistic mapping to the training, or support, set. In this way, neural networks, including large pre-trained networks, can be viewed as <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hidden</span> instance-based metric learners, from which we can then derive signals of the epistemic uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Exemplar Adaptor</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take as the CNN of our exemplar adaptor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mapsto{\bm{h}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mapsto{\bm{h}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, a 1-D CNN that takes as input <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the underlying network. The CNN has <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> filters, the filter applications of which produce <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the distilled representation of the underlying network. A final linear layer, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{\prime T}{\bm{h}}^{\prime}+{\bm{b}}^{\prime},{\bm{z}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{\prime T}{\bm{h}}^{\prime}+{\bm{b}}^{\prime},{\bm{z}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then replaces the underlying network’s linear layer, with the discrete prediction taken as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This exemplar adaptor will then enable us to derive the key signals of epistemic uncertainty, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> described next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span><math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>) of an instance to the training set as the count of consecutive nearest matches in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> that are correctly predicted <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> match <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> of the test instance.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              We use the letter <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>, as this value <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">q</span>uantizes the closeness of a point to the training set with a discrete estimate.</span></span></span> Concretely, we first sort <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{\rm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{\rm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> (for which we have both model predictions and ground-truth labels) based on the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance (2-norm) from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(1)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(1)},y^{tr}_{(1)}),\ldots,({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(N)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(N)},y^{tr}_{(N)})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(1)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(1)},y^{tr}_{(1)}),\ldots,({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(N)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(N)},y^{tr}_{(N)})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}\leq\ldots\leq||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(N)}||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}\leq\ldots\leq||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(N)}||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then calculate <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{0,\ldots,|\trainSplit|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{0,\ldots,|\trainSplit|\}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\sum^{|\trainSplit|}_{i=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{i-1=\sum^{i-1}_{j=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.980em">tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\sum^{|\trainSplit|}_{i=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{i-1=\sum^{i-1}_{j=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the rightmost indicator function, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensures consecutive (depth-wise) matches.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              This seemingly simple rule differs from traditional KNN rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cover &amp; Hart, 1967</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devroye et al., 1996</a>, inter alia)</cite> in two critical respects: The neural network serves as a semi-supervised metric learner of the distances between the dense representations that identify the instances, and there is a model prediction (in addition to the ground-truth label) for each instance in the support set. The former enables effective partitioning despite the curse of high dimensions; the latter provides an additional indicator of reliability for each instance.</span></span></span> By definition, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> cannot exceed the count of the most prevalent class label in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, and since we assume an approximately equal number of points for each class, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q\ll|\trainSplit|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q\ll|\trainSplit|</annotation></semantics></math> is typical. For the special case of calculating <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, which only occurs during learning, we exclude the self-match.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3 </span><math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance to the nearest match in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> follows from above: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest=||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>tr</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest=||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We normalize these values by defining the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, in terms of the class-wise empirical CDFs of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, as the most conservative quantile relative to the distance to the nearest matches observed in the labeled, held-out set:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal=\min\left[1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest),\ldots,1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal=\min\left[1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest),\ldots,1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The empirical CDFs are determined by the labeled points in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, where, as indicated by the superscripts, the stratification of points is by the true labels, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical CDF of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> values in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>, a notation convention we will use throughout. (Points with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q=0</annotation></semantics></math> are effectively out-of-distribution points and treated as such in downstream decision-making, so they are excluded to avoid biasing the estimates.) At test time, we do not see <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>; instead, the minimum is calculated over the quantiles of each of the class-conditional eCDFs, regardless of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. As with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, for the special case of calculating <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the analogous <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>tr</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the class-wise empirical CDFs of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> excluding self-matches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.4 </span><math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take as the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>, or distance to the decision boundary, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as in the standard <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> case but via <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the linear layer of the exemplar adaptor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.5 </span>SDM Activation: Formulation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the above quantities to define the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation function:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{i}=\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{i}=\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The output distribution becomes sharper with higher values of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also note that when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the largest distance observed in the labeled data, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal=0</annotation></semantics></math> and the output distribution is uniform, reflecting a maximally high (i.e., out-of-distribution) epistemic uncertainty estimate. The standard <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered by setting <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As with the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.6 </span>SDM Activation: Loss and Training</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A loss analogous to Eq. <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> then follows with the applicable change of base. We use this loss to train the weights of the exemplar adaptor, which includes the parameters of the linear layer (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), as well as the convolution weights and biases, which we collectively represent with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weights of the underlying <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> remain fixed.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{G}},{\bm{W}}^{\prime},{\bm{b}}^{\prime};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{(2+q)}\left(\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{G}},{\bm{W}}^{\prime},{\bm{b}}^{\prime};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{(2+q)}\left(\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first epoch of training is initialized with a standard <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., setting <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Training then proceeds by re-calculating <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> after each epoch. We take as the stopping criteria for one learning round as the epoch with the highest balanced (across classes) average loss over <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We repeat this process for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of random shuffles and splits of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and parameter initializations, choosing the final model as that with the globally highest balanced (across classes) average loss over <math id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Evaluating Selective Classification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a common, unambiguous baseline quantity for comparing selective classifiers, we seek a straightforward balance between precision and recall. Among the selective classifications from an estimator, we seek (I) prediction-conditional accuracy and (II) class-conditional accuracy above a given threshold, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(\frac{1}{C},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(\frac{1}{C},1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate this quantity, we only consider the points for which the given estimator assigns a high-probability of at least <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which is typically near 1, such as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.95</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments. We refer to this set of points as the <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">admitted</span>, or <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-rejected</span>, set. Then, given ground-truth values for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, we assess whether the conditional accuracies of the admitted set are at least <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> when (I) stratifying by the predicted labels, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and when (II) stratifying by the true labels, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The estimator that rejects all points would meet these conditions. However, given two estimators that meet these conditions, we prefer that which rejects fewer points, ceteris paribus. In other words, we seek estimators that meet our reliability condition and are informative (i.e., maximize the number of points that are properly admitted), but when the estimator is uncertain, we prefer rejection over unexpectedly falling under the desired <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> probability threshold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Quantity I corresponds to top-label calibration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta &amp; Ramdas, 2022</a>)</cite>, but with a single bin for evaluation, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, removing ambiguity with regard to the choice of binning the probabilities. Quantity II does not directly correspond to quantities typically examined in the empirical calibration literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brier, 1950</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dawid, 1982</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaicenavicius et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kull et al., 2019</a>, inter alia)</cite>, but it approximates<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            For simplicity of presentation, we omit consideration of the Beta-distributed error term that is a function of the effective sample size of split-conformal coverage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vovk, 2012</a>)</cite>. In practice, this term is negligible in the present experiments given <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\calibrationSplit|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\calibrationSplit|</annotation></semantics></math> and the resolution of the comparisons.</span></span></span> label-conditional conformal coverage in the restricted setting of class-wise thresholds that only admit prediction sets of cardinality 1. We describe a straightforward procedure to estimate this quantity next.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Controlling Selective Classification Recall</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, the statistical coverage assurance of marginal split-conformal estimators does not directly correspond to selection, since the coverage assurance is not conditional on the set size. We may instead seek one of various approximately conditional notions of coverage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Romano et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al., 2021</a>, inter alia)</cite>; however, there is no guarantee that when we stratify by sets of cardinality 1, coverage will be maintained. However, there is a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">restricted setting</span> in which label-conditional conformal estimators do provide a meaningful notion of class-conditional coverage for selection. Assuming <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are exchangeable, if the conformity score for each label is from a categorical distribution and the resulting thresholding of the class-wise empirical CDFs results in class-wise thresholds that are all greater than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cardinality 1 sets will, on average, obtain class-conditional coverage, by definition.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
              Although the formal interpretations are not identical, the evaluation of class-conditional coverage for a single estimate of such cardinality 1 prediction sets in this restricted setting over <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math> is numerically equivalent to assessing the class-conditional accuracy, when not considering the sample size error term.</span></span></span> Unfortunately, it may be rare to encounter this restricted setting over the full data distributions of real-world tasks. Instead, we will use the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation to estimate a partitioning of the distribution into a region that approximately fulfills these assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we rescale the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math> estimate to take into account the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>, given the predicted class. The resulting value<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
              The <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that <math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math> instances remain 0.</span></span></span> will be the basis for partitioning the distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}=\min\left(\q,(2+q)^{\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}=\min\left(\q,(2+q)^{\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we estimate label-conditional conformal thresholds, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math>, over the output from the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation among a subset of the distribution determined by progressively larger values of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (among <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor{q^{\prime}}\right\rfloor&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor{q^{\prime}}\right\rfloor&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) until all thresholds are at least <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. By setting our stopping criteria at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also restrict the region to our empirically-motivated prediction-conditional quantity, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The procedure appears in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. If we find a finite <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> that obtains such thresholds, we refer to the resulting region as the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hrRegionFull" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">high-reliability</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hrRegionFull</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">HR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}</annotation></semantics></math>) region, taking membership in this region as our selection criteria:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}:=\begin{cases}\hat{y}&amp;\text{if~}q^{\prime}\geq q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}\wedge\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}\geq\psi_{\hat{y}}\\
\bot&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">HR</mtext></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mo>⊥</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}:=\begin{cases}\hat{y}&amp;\text{if~}q^{\prime}\geq q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}\wedge\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}\geq\psi_{\hat{y}}\\
\bot&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a rejected (non-admitted) point and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To calculate this quantity for new, unseen test points <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, we require <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> to calculate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math>; the cached class-wise empirical CDFs of the distances over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title='In 3.1.3 "Distance" ‣ 3.1 Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activation Functions ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>; and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the thresholds (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math>). Evaluation of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">HR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}</annotation></semantics></math> selection criteria is straightforward: We simply assess the conditional accuracies for the admitted points after stratifying by the predictions and the true labels, each in turn.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> returns <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a useful empirical indicator that the model is too weak, or the data insufficient, to reliably obtain class- and prediction-conditional estimates at the specified <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> value.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Search Algorithm to Find <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> to Determine the <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hrRegionFull" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">high-reliability</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hrRegionFull</annotation></semantics></math> Region</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">cached </span><math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q^{\prime},\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps" mathsize="0.900em">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q^{\prime},\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(\frac{1}{C},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(\frac{1}{C},1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">procedure</span><span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">find-min-rescaled-similarity</span><span id="alg1.l2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(cached </span><math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q^{\prime},\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps" mathsize="0.900em">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q^{\prime},\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for </span><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(\frac{1}{C},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(\frac{1}{C},1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}\leftarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}\leftarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> A finite <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> may not be found.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\leftarrow[\infty,\ldots,\infty]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\leftarrow[\infty,\ldots,\infty]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{sortedList}~\leftarrow{\rm{sorted}}~[q^{\prime}\in\calibrationSplit~{\rm{s.t.}}~\left\lfloor{q^{\prime}}\right\rfloor&gt;0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">sortedList</mtext><mo lspace="0.588em" mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">sorted</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.310em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.477em">.</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sortedList}~\leftarrow{\rm{sorted}}~[q^{\prime}\in\calibrationSplit~{\rm{s.t.}}~\left\lfloor{q^{\prime}}\right\rfloor&gt;0]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg1.l5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Restricted to <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor{q^{\prime}}\right\rfloor&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor{q^{\prime}}\right\rfloor&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> to exclude OOD
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="alg1.l6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q^{*}\in\text{sortedList}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">sortedList</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q^{*}\in\text{sortedList}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span><span id="alg1.l6.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   Construct </span><math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}},\ldots,{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">eCDF</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">eCDF</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}},\ldots,{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> for all </span><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}\geq\q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}\geq\q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> in </span><math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> eCDFs for <math id="alg1.l7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1.5 SDM Activation: Formulation ‣ 3.1 Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activation Functions ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), stratified by <math id="alg1.l7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   Calculate </span><math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">inverseCDF</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ca</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.477em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.477em">∀</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.588em" mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{c}={\rm{inverseCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}(1-\alpha^{\prime})~\forall~c~\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Quantile functions are inverses of L. <a href="#alg1.l7" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">if</span><span id="alg1.l9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{all}}(~[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\geq\alpha~)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">all</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.310em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.310em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{all}}(~[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]\geq\alpha~)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">then</span><span id="alg1.l9.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l9.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;float:right;"><math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Element-wise comparison
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">      </span><math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}\leftarrow\q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}\leftarrow\q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">      </span><span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">break</span><span id="alg1.l11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
     </span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">return</span><span id="alg1.l12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l12.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l12.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">13:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">min</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\psi_{1},\ldots\psi_{C}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide controlled comparisons of our proposed methods over representative LLMs and tasks, systematically ablating relevant components, holding the data and underlying LLM constant, ceteris paribus. We consider in-distribution, co-variate shifted, and out-of-distribution test sets. We consider representative estimators over the existing LLM architecture (i.e., without additional parameters); with CNN adaptors; and with the <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Task: Binary Sentiment Classification</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first task is predicting the sentiment of movie reviews. We use the commonly used benchmark data of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maas et al. (2011)</a></cite>. This is a binary classification task with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>negative</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>positive</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are constructed from a total of 18k instances. The held-out set for evaluation, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=1583" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1583</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=1583</annotation></semantics></math>, is from the same distribution as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a well-studied task for which the surface-level signals correlated with the target labels are expected to be effectively modeled by large parameter LLMs; as such, relatively high task accuracies are expected. We use the label <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math> for the in-distribution test set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the behavior of the estimators over out-of-distribution (OOD) data, we consider an additional evaluation set, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=4750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=4750</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the SemEval-2017 Task 4a test set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rosenthal et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, which consists of short-form social media posts that differ in the distribution of topics, language styles, and lengths relative to the movie reviews. We balance the test set, dropping the third class (neutral), setting the semantics of the true labels to be the same as that of the movie reviews: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>negative</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>positive</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentShuffled</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOODShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOODShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOODShuffled</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the Appendix, we consider two additional out-of-distribution challenge test sets, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentShuffled</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOODShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOODShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOODShuffled</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed by randomly shuffling the input documents for each of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The semantics of the original labels are maintained. This requires the models and estimators to attempt a sentiment classification over the bag-of-words input, or reject the classification. This represents the setting where an LLM is given far out-of-distribution input, and additionally provides a control on test-set contamination of the underlying LLMs, which due to the shuffling, are relatively unlikely to have seen all of the long, contiguous n-gram sequences from these documents in training or fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For this task, we prompt the LLMs for a binary classification (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> or <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span>) as follows: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Here is a movie review. &lt;review&gt; <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">DOCUMENT</span> &lt;/review&gt; Is the sentiment of the movie review positive? Answer Yes if the sentiment is positive. Answer No if the sentiment is negative. Start your response with Yes or No.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We replace <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_typewriter">DOCUMENT</span> with the corresponding text for each instance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Task: <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a more challenging binary classification task for LLMs, we consider the fact check data of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azaria &amp; Mitchell (2023)</a></cite>. The training and calibration sets, a combined total of 6k instances, consist of single sentence statements that have been semi-automatically generated via templates and a knowledge base. The task is to determine whether the statement is true or false, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0={\rm{false}},1={\rm{true}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>false</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>true</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0={\rm{false}},1={\rm{true}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The held-out eval set (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=245" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>245</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=245</annotation></semantics></math>, the focus of our analysis, has been constructed by having an LLM generate a statement continued from a true statement not otherwise in the dataset. These evaluation statements are checked manually and assigned labels by human annotators. In addition to being a relatively challenging task that evaluates—at least in principle—the latent knowledge stored within an LLM’s parameters, the test set is representative of the types of co-variate shifts over high-dimensional inputs that can be problematic for real applications, and challenging to characterize without model assistance and ground-truth labels. It was observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azaria &amp; Mitchell (2023)</a></cite> that the accuracy of existing LLM classifiers is lower on this generated, held-out test set compared to the calibration set. However, these test sentences would seem to also be simple true-false statements, reflecting that it is not always immediately obvious for a human user to detect distribution shifts over high-dimensional inputs. As such, we seek for our models and estimators to reflect such shifts via the predictive uncertainty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheckShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FactcheckShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheckShuffled</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As with the sentiment task, in the Appendix we also consider an additional out-of-distribution challenge test set, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheckShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FactcheckShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheckShuffled</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed by randomly shuffling the input documents of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the task prompt (below) seeks to classify errors, we set the ground-truth labels of the shuffled counterparts to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the sentiment task, we prompt the LLMs for a binary classification (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> or <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span>) as follows: <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Here is a statement that may contain errors. &lt;statement&gt; <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">DOCUMENT</span> &lt;/statement&gt; Is the statement true? Answer Yes if the statement is true. Answer No if the statement is false. Start your response with Yes or No.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We replace <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_typewriter">DOCUMENT</span> with the corresponding text for each instance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider two representative, publicly-available decoder-only Transformer-based language models: The 3.8 billion-parameter <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Phi-3.5-mini-instruct</span> model (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and the 47 billion-parameter <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mixtral 8x7B Instruct v0.1</span> mixture-of-experts model (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelMixtral" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Mixtral8x7B</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelMixtral</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The parameters of these models stay fixed in all experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hidden-states.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For both models, we take as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> the concatenation of the final-layer hidden state of the final sequence position (i.e., the hidden state that is the input to the linear-layer over the output vocabulary for the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> or <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span> generation) with the sequence-wise mean over all final-layer hidden states. For <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct</annotation></semantics></math>, this results in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{6144}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6144</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{6144}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelMixtral" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Mixtral8x7B</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelMixtral</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{8192}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>8192</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{8192}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Estimators</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Holding each underlying LLM constant, we examine representative classes of estimators used with neural networks, setting <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.95</annotation></semantics></math> for all experiments. At the most basic, but also, perhaps the most commonly used in practice, representing the absence of a post-hoc calibration method, we simply threshold the output, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}})\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}})\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where the temperature <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As an established empirical approach for calibrating neural networks, we provide a comparison to temperature scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, a single parameter version of post-hoc Platt-scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Platt, 1999</a>)</cite>, with the label <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorTempScaling" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">tempScaling</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorTempScaling</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the estimator is the thresholding of the output <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}};\tau)\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}};\tau)\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> after learning a value for <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We also provide a comparison to two representative conformal predictors, the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">APS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalAPS</annotation></semantics></math> method of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Romano et al. (2020)</a></cite> and the adaptiveness-optimized <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalRAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">RAPS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalRAPS</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al. (2021)</a></cite>. The admission criteria for the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">APS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalAPS</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalRAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">RAPS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalRAPS</annotation></semantics></math> estimators is prediction sets of size 1, at the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> level (i.e., <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined here). We consider these estimators over the logits corresponding to the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span> indexes of the output linear-layer of the underlying LLM (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelMixtral" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Mixtral8x7B</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelMixtral</annotation></semantics></math>), which provides a reference point without introducing additional adaptor layers. We also consider these baselines over 1-D CNN adaptors over <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of each LLM (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructCNNAdaptor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+adaptor</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructCNNAdaptor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelMixtralCNNAdaptor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Mixtral8x7B+adaptor</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelMixtralCNNAdaptor</annotation></semantics></math>). These baseline adaptors are identical to those used in the corresponding <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layers, with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1000</annotation></semantics></math>, and are similarly trained for <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=10</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of 200 epochs, but unlike the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layers, the stopping criteria is the maximum balanced (across classes) average cross-entropy loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then compare to the final-layer <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activations over <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of each LLM (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDM</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelMixtralSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Mixtral8x7B+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelMixtralSDM</annotation></semantics></math>). For reference, we provide the result of thresholding a <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> over these adaptors at <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, as above, as well as a thresholded <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> that simply treats <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal</annotation></semantics></math> as the inverse-temperature, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorSoftmax(\dVal\cdot{\bm{z}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">softmax</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorSoftmax(\dVal\cdot{\bm{z}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to setting <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e-2</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation. We also consider an analogous threshold over the activation output, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, for which we use <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> as the estimator label. Finally, we use the label <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">HR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}</annotation></semantics></math> for the selection criteria proposed in Eq. <a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a common point of reference, the label <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorNoReject" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">no-reject</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorNoReject</annotation></semantics></math> refers to predictions without any selective filtering (i.e., the raw output accuracies, derived from the <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math> over the final linear-layer).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">In-distribution data.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results for the in-distribution <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math> dataset appear in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ In-distribution data. ‣ 5 Results ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Even on this in-distribution dataset, the estimators over the underlying LLMs without adaptor layers exhibit modest over-confidence, which is reflected in conditional accuracies that fall below the expected <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The estimators over the adaptor layers and the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation all obtain the desired conditional accuracies, with the class-wise accuracies of the models themselves <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, with differences arising in the proportion of admitted points. Here and elsewhere, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorSoftmax(\dVal\cdot{\bm{z}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">softmax</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorSoftmax(\dVal\cdot{\bm{z}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> tends to be overly conservative in rejecting points. To be expected, the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">HR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}</annotation></semantics></math> estimator tends to be more conservative than simply thresholding the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation on the in-distribution data, but the latter lacks the assurances on the recall obtained by the constraints on the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hrRegionFull" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">high-reliability</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hrRegionFull</annotation></semantics></math> region. In practice, this behavior can be used as a basis to triage selective classifications: For example, documents in the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hrRegionFull" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">high-reliability</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hrRegionFull</annotation></semantics></math> region might be treated as automated, or semi-automated, predictions in the decision pipeline, whereas other documents might be triaged by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> for calling more resource-intensive LLM tools, or human adjudication. Importantly, as we discuss below with the co-variate-shifted and out-of-distribution datasets, just using the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> or the other estimators does not provide a reliable substrate for basing such conditional branching decisions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of estimators for the sentiment datasets, with <span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text"><math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;" mathbackground="#F2FFF2">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span><math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">R</span> indicates all predictions were rejected, which is preferred over falling <span id="S5.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF2F2;">under</span> the expected accuracy.
