<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Co-variate-shifted and Out-of-distribution data.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bottom half of Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ In-distribution data. ‣ 5 Results ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides the results for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, the distinctions between the estimators become clear, with the non-<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math>-based estimators performing poorly, even in terms of the marginal accuracy. That would come as a surprise to end-users, whereas with the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">HR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm_{\hrRegionShort}</annotation></semantics></math> estimators, the out-of-distribution documents are more reliably rejected, with the few admitted predictions generally obtaining high conditional accuracies, despite the relatively low accuracies of the test set without selection (see <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorNoReject" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">no-reject</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorNoReject</annotation></semantics></math>). A similar pattern is observed over the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math> dataset in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Far out-of-distribution data. ‣ 5 Results ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Understanding <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For reference, Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Far out-of-distribution data. ‣ 5 Results ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> provides the results over <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math>-based estimates. The value of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> tends to increase as the accuracy over <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, reflecting a more conservative <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hrRegionFull" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">high-reliability</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hrRegionFull</annotation></semantics></math> region that admits fewer points. Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> failed to find a finite <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelMixtralSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Mixtral8x7B+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelMixtralSDM</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math> calibration set at <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, so for reference, we also show the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hrRegionFull" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">high-reliability</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hrRegionFull</annotation></semantics></math> region at <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.94</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.94</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Far out-of-distribution data. ‣ 5 Results ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In this way, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}_{\rm{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a principled, data-driven indicator of the reliability of the estimates, which is interpretable as a simple indicator as to whether the conditional accuracies are, or are not, obtainable over <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> at the specified <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Far out-of-distribution data.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Appendix provides Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.1 Far OOD Shuffled Datasets ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.1 Far OOD Shuffled Datasets ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> with analogous results for the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentShuffled</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOODShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOODShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOODShuffled</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheckShuffled" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FactcheckShuffled</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheckShuffled</annotation></semantics></math> datasets. The selection criteria of Eq. <a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reliably rejects the challenging predictions, whereas the non-<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math>-based estimators fare poorly, in general. In this way, the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation serves as an effective out-of-distribution detection method. With existing methods, defining an out-of-distribution point has been task- and problem-specific, and generally challenging over high-dimensional inputs, typically requiring additional modeling beyond that of the calibration or selection method. In contrast, Eq. <a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2.1 Controlling Selective Classification Recall ‣ 3.2 Evaluating Selective Classification ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> provides a principled approach for determining such cut-offs in a data- and model-driven manner, with minimal hyper-parameters, resulting in a separation of points over which the estimator tends to be reliable (namely, the admitted points) and those over which the estimates themselves tend to be unreliable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of estimators for the factcheck datasets. Unless specified otherwise, <span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text"><math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;" mathbackground="#F2FFF2">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span><math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">R</span> indicates all predictions were rejected, which is preferred over falling <span id="S5.T2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF2F2;">under</span> the expected accuracy.
<math id="S5.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|\text{Admitted}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>Admitted</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|\text{Admitted}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the count of non-rejected documents.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:469.8pt;height:340.1pt;vertical-align:-168.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-56.6pt,40.9pt) scale(0.805933193165711,0.805933193165711) ;">
