<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation functions, which provide more robust estimators of the predictive uncertainty than the commonly-used <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> function. In this way, <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activations provide a principled, data-driven substrate for approaching selective classification, calibration, and out-of-distribution detection with LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
